THE REGION BADGES -- BG Wiki, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0
----------------------------------------------
  assets/category/*.png         17 files, generated by tools/make_category_icons.py

These are the category images BG Wiki uses on its own Category:Quests and
Category:Missions pages. The quest list groups by 31 region keys, of which 28
carry a badge, and those 28 share these 17 images between them because the wiki
shares them: San d'Oria's crest serves its quests and its missions, and Rise of
the Zilart has no image of its own, so the wiki uses Jeuno's. Which image
belongs to which category is the wiki's own pairing, read from the anchor each
image sits inside on those two pages rather than chosen here. Three regions get
no badge: quest/coalition, mission/assault and mission/nation.

  Source:   https://www.bg-wiki.com
  Licence:  CC BY-NC-SA 3.0  --  https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/

That licence carries three obligations, and all three apply to this addon:

  BY   -- attribution, which this file provides. assets/category/SOURCES.json
          additionally records, per shipped file, the BG Wiki filename it came
          from and every wiki category that uses it.
  NC   -- non-commercial use only. This addon is given away and sold nowhere.
  SA   -- derivative works under the same licence. The shipped badges ARE
          derivative works: tools/make_category_icons.py downscales each
          original to 64 px and, where it can do so without touching the
          artwork, lifts the flat plate out from behind it. SOURCES.json records
          which of the two happened to each file. They are therefore offered
          under CC BY-NC-SA 3.0, and so is any further derivative of them.

  The originals as downloaded are not in the repository, because they are BG
  Wiki's files rather than this addon's and they are re-fetchable. What ships is
  the record of them: tools/category-sources/icons/manifest.json names the source
  URL of every one, tools/category-sources/fetch_icons.py is what downloads them
  again, and tools/category-sources/README.md records how: a descriptive
  User-Agent, one request at a time and a second between them, following the
  conventions questmarks' own fetcher established.

  A LIMIT WORTH STATING PLAINLY. The wiki's licence covers the wiki's content.
  Most of these images are Square Enix promotional or in-game artwork uploaded
  to the wiki, and SQUARE ENIX's copyright in that artwork is a separate
  question from BG Wiki's CC licence over its pages. A fan wiki cannot license
  someone else's artwork to you. What makes this defensible is the same thing
  that makes the rest of the addon defensible: it is unofficial,
  non-commercial, unsold, and it identifies the game it serves. It is not a
  claim that the artwork is free of Square Enix's rights.


WHAT WAS READ AND NOT COPIED
----------------------------
chronicle/ui/ was read, to learn how a quest browser is built on this platform.
No code and no data comes from it. That phrasing is questmarks' own, which says
of Balloon that "it was READ, to learn how rendered NPC dialogue reaches an
addon. No code or data comes from it", and it is exact here.

It matters that chronicle ships no LICENSE file, and that its BSD-3-Clause
header sits on chronicle.lua alone and on none of the three files under
chronicle/ui/ this design learned from. Reading for technique is safe on those
terms; copying would not have been.
